8T17:13:14Z"},"comments":2,"created_at":"2025-05-14T05:17:44Z","updated_at":"2025-05-14T17:35:57Z","closed_at":"2025-05-14T17:35:56Z","assignee":null,"author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","issue_field_values":[],"type":null,"active_lock_reason":null,"sub_issues_summary":{"total":0,"completed":0,"percent_completed":0},"issue_dependencies_summary":{"blocked_by":0,"total_blocked_by":0,"blocking":0,"total_blocking":0},"body":"### Tested versions\n\n4.5-dev4 (game window embedding on macOS is new in this version)\n\n### System information\n\nGodot v4.5.dev4 - macOS Sequoia (15.4.1) - Multi-window (also reproduced on single-window), 2 monitors - Metal (Forward+) - integrated Apple M1 Max (Apple7) - Apple M1 Max (10 threads)\n\n### Issue description\n\nWhen there is a visible mesh onscreen whose `StandardMaterial3D` has `subsurf_scatter_enabled` set to `true`, the 3D scene renders incorrectly _only_ when running in embedded window mode.\n\nWhen this is the case, much of the scene will be invisible or miscolored, and what _is_ visible interacts with the OS strangely: it does not appear in screen captures, and is only visible on the primary monitor.\n\nHere is a photograph of the incorrect behavior:\n![Image](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/c9dc3fc0-e6b0-403e-8b94-7453f0e6b057)\n\nFor reference, here is a screenshot of the expected behavior, captured from a non-embedded game window:\n![Image](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/d3764707-6e6f-47fc-863e-007141b7a7b4)\n\nHere is a photograph demonstrating how the visible portions are invisible on the secondary monitor:\n![Image](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/9f3c0474-166b-41c1-a8cd-1d8d1e9226f8)\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\nRun the MRP's default scene, embedding the game window.\n\nNote the incorrect rendering.\n(Compare to the behavior when the game window is not embedded.)\n\nNote that removing the subsurf-scattering mesh returns rendering to normal, even in an embedded game window.\n\n### Minimal 